  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal temps through Saturday.\n\nRidge aloft to linger into the end of the week, though some\nsuppression comes by the weekend with a backdoor front. Still\ntemps should run well above normal especially Wed-Thu,\napproaching triple digits in the Piedmont. Dewpoints and cloud\ncover may be key in whether heat advisory levels of 105 are\nreached in the Piedmont. Based on the latest model trends,\nappears to be just under for Wed-Thu, so will hold off on any\nadvisory. Still heat this time of year is bad, so make sure you\ntake extra precautions if outdoors.\n\nA back door cold front may move through this weekend and bring \nus back down to highs in the mid-70s to mid-80s by early next \nweek.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Showers/storms possible Thu evening into the\nweekend. Some could be strong/severe especially Friday.\n\nAs the ridge sinks further southwest Thu into Friday, models\nhave some shortwave/vort centers tracking across the Great Lakes\ninto the mid-Atlantic. We may get a piece of that shortwave Thu\nevening across our eastern CWA if the storm clusters sink\nfurther southeast. Most models have it dry but still potential\nexists.\n\nFriday looks slightly better especially in the northern CWA\nsince the flow turns more nnw-sse and allows for some storm\nclusters to drive southeast into the Shenandoah Valley and\ncentral Piedmont. National Blend of Models has been keeping on\ndry side, but have upped pops for this contingency.\n\nSurface CAPES should exceed 1500 J/kg and bulk shear of \n20-35kts appears sufficient for potential winds to become an \nissue Friday afternoon/evening.\n\nFront tracks south through the area this weekend keeping chances\nof showers around, but with a little more cloud cover, temps\nwill cool. High pressure wedging early next week should dry\nthings out.",
